{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Deliver me</b> — Rabbi Marinus, of blessed memory, said: כי אליך כסיתי (for I have covered [myself] to You) is like בך חסיתי (in You I have taken refuge). And the correct interpretation in my eyes: the word לקיחה (taking) will carry the opposite meaning on account of the letter after it, like קחו מאתכם תרומה (take from among you a contribution). And the meaning is like תנו קחו לי (give, take for me), that he should take for his own need. And thus סורה אלי (turn aside to me), that it should turn from its place and come to him; and the opposite of this is סורה ממני (turn aside from me). If so, the word כסוי (covering): if he said המכסה אני מאברהם (am I covering [myself] from Abraham), it is clear; and if he said המכסה אני אל אברהם (am I covering [myself] to Abraham), the matter is reversed." "And the meaning of הצילני מאויבי ה' (deliver me, O YHWH, from my enemies) — כי אליך כסיתי (for to You I have covered), that I reveal them to You in secret. And behold, the linguistic correctness: כסיתי (I have covered), this matter from others, for I have no need to reveal to them; they cannot help me. Only to You alone, for You are able to deliver me."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 143, :notes {0 "The examples סורה אלי and סורה ממני seem to invoke 2 Samuel 18:30 and similar forms; the argument about directionality of the preposition is preserved as Ibn Ezra gives it.", 1 "The reading מאיבי ה' in the heading reflects the Hebrew comment's citation form; the psalm verse reads מאויבי (from my enemies) with the divine name following. Also, the switch in [1] from כסיתי meaning 'taken refuge' to 'covered/concealed' is Ibn Ezra's own re-reading, preserved literally."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:42:44.261664816Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}